  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1255 PM EDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast: Amplified pattern continues across NOAM \nwith several upper waves noted upstream -- in particular one across \nSaskatchewan/Manitoba, one over the desert southwest, and a third \noff the coast of British Columbia/Pac NW. Upper ridging currently \noverhead locally will break down with time late in the weekend into \nthe start of next week as that northernmost wave treks across \nnorthern Ontario/Hudson Bay into northern Quebec. Second wave over \nthe southwest will make progress into the north-central CONUS \nTuesday. The third wave not arriving until late week, likely being \nabsorbed into more broad troughing, eventually expected to sag \nacross the nation's midsection and provide our best chance at rain \nlocally.\n\nForecast Details: Little in the way of sensible weather through at \nleast late tonight as upper ridging dominates aloft and we remain \nunder the influence of surface high pressure centered over the \nNortheast. This upper pattern begins to break down late tonight into \nSunday as vertically stacked low pressure treks by well to our \nnorth. This will drag an elongated cold front near northern MI late \ntonight/Sunday morning and eventually across the area Sunday night. \nForcing/moisture with this feature locally are lacking, to say the \nleast, but low chances for showers do enter the forecast across \nparts of the CWA as early as Sunday morning with those chances \nlingering in spots through Sunday evening. High temperatures Sunday \nlargely in the 60s area-wide, with the low 70s possible in \ndownsloping areas of northeast lower. Post-frontal cooler \ntemperatures progged for Monday and Tuesday with highs back in the \nupper 40s far north and through the 50s over northern lower -- \ncertainly closer to late October normals.\n\nShower chances Tuesday looking likely to stay primarily off to our \nwest with that secondary wave driving into the northern plains. Next \nshower threat locally looking to hold off until Wednesday night into \nThursday. Doesn't look like a complete washout, but certainly \nfeatures higher precip chances than we've seen in the last 7-10+ \ndays. \n\nLooking toward Halloween weekend, previously progged cool down now \nlooking pretty transient overall. Low-level thermal trough sags in \nbriefly Thursday night - Friday before quickly retreating back to \nthe north. Above normal temperatures anticipated for Halloween day, \nwith latest trends supporting low-mid 60s over northern lower and a \ntouch cooler north of the bridge. Rain chances may become more \nfrequent next weekend as well, but lots of spatial/temporal \nuncertainty at this point.",
